# DNS Daddy — Docker Compose environment.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   $EDITOR .env
#   docker compose up -d
#
# .env is gitignored: it holds addresses specific to your deployment.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who may use this resolver
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave this commented out for a home, homelab or LAN install. There is
# nothing to do.
#
# The built-in default already serves loopback, every RFC 1918 private range,
# carrier-grade NAT (what Tailscale hands out), link-local, and the IPv6
# equivalents — and the Docker bridge, so a published port keeps working.
# None of those are routable from the public internet.
#
# Setting this REPLACES that default outright; it does not add to it. Setting
# it to less than your LAN is the single most common way to end up with a
# resolver that reports itself healthy while every client gets REFUSED. If you
# do not need to change it, do not change it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# You DO need to set it on a public VPS.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There your clients arrive from public addresses, which the default does not
# cover, so every real query is refused. List the same source addresses your
# firewall allows on port 53, and keep:
#
#   127.0.0.0/8      loopback, for health checks
#   172.16.0.0/12    the Docker bridge, or the published port breaks
#
# Example for two sites:
# DNSDADDY_ALLOWED_CLIENT_CIDRS=127.0.0.0/8,172.16.0.0/12,203.0.113.42/32,198.51.100.10/32
#
# Whatever you set, `dnsdaddy doctor` will tell you whether the networks in
# your dashboard can actually reach the resolver.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reaching the dashboard
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# By default the dashboard is published on 127.0.0.1:8080 — reachable from the
# server itself and nowhere else.
#
# ON A MACHINE WITH NO PUBLIC ADDRESS — a home server, a LAN VM, a box behind
# your router — set this to its own LAN address to open http://<that
# address>:8080 from your laptop. Find it with `ip -4 addr` or `hostname -I`.
#
# DNSDADDY_DASHBOARD_BIND=192.168.1.75
#
# CHECK THIS IS NOT A CLOUD VPS FIRST. On AWS, GCP, Azure, Hetzner Cloud and
# most providers the instance holds a private address and the public one is
# NATed onto it, so `ip addr` shows 10.x or 172.x while the machine is fully
# reachable from the internet. Binding the dashboard to that private address
# publishes an authenticated but *plaintext* management API to the world. A
# private address on the NIC is not evidence that a host is private.
#
# Do NOT set this to 0.0.0.0 either, and note that Docker's port publishing
# bypasses ufw so a firewall rule will not contain it. On any host with a
# public address, leave this alone and use an SSH tunnel or put TLS in front —
# see the reverse proxy section below.
#
# If it does go wrong, DNS Daddy says so: a management request arriving over
# plain HTTP from a public address is recorded and raised on the dashboard and
# at /api/v1/diagnostics.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reverse proxy (leave blank if the dashboard is loopback-only with no proxy)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Peers whose X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP / X-Forwarded-Proto are believed.
#
# Counter-intuitive but important: with Caddy on the host proxying to
# 127.0.0.1:8080, the container does NOT see 127.0.0.1. Docker's port
# publishing rewrites the source to the bridge gateway, so this must be the
# bridge network — not loopback.
#
# Find yours:  docker network inspect bridge -f '{{(index .IPAM.Config 0).Subnet}}'
# DNSDADDY_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=172.17.0.0/16

# Session cookie Secure flag: auto | always | never.
# Set "always" once TLS is in front, so it can never cross plain HTTP.
# DNSDADDY_SECURE_COOKIES=always

# Public URL, used to build the DoH URLs shown on the Setup page.
# DNSDADDY_BASE_URL=https://dns.example.co.uk

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Behavioural detection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On by default. Nothing here blocks anything — the detectors observe, score,
# explain and alert; enforcement stays with policy and threat feeds. Every
# detector is experimental. See docs/detection/README.md.
#
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_ENABLED=true

# info | low | medium | high. Findings below this are not stored.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_MIN_SEVERITY=low

# Write findings as newline-delimited JSON for a log shipper to tail. A
# relative path lands inside the data volume. Off by default: this is browsing
# history in a second place. See docs/siem.md.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_FINDINGS_FILE=findings.jsonl

# Parent domains exempt from behavioural findings, on top of the built-in list.
# Reputation services, CDNs and mail gateways legitimately produce traffic
# shaped exactly like a DNS tunnel. This never affects whether a domain
# resolves.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_EXCLUDED_DOMAINS=reputation.your-vendor.example,gateway.your-filter.example

# How long findings are kept. They outlive the query log on purpose.
# DNSDADDY_DETECTION_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Threat feeds
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The DNS Daddy Threat Observatory (threats.dnsdaddy.dev) ships in the catalog
# DISABLED, so a stock install fetches nothing from a server we run. Enable it
# on the Threat feeds page if you want it; docs/threat-intel.md says exactly
# what that exposes (your server's IP and refresh cadence, never your queries).
# There is nothing to configure here: what it blocks is decided by the
# categories your policies enable, like every other feed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TZ=UTC
# DNSDADDY_VERSION=dev
